.TH CERTMONGER 8 "October 25, 2026" "certmonger Manual"

.SH NAME
est\-submit

.SH SYNOPSIS
est\-submit \-u SERVER\-URL
[\-i label]
[\-R ca\-cert\-file]
[\-U username]
[\-W password\-file]
[\-v]
[\-C|\-e|\-r]
[csr\-filename]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fIest\-submit\fR is the helper which \fIcertmonger\fR can use to
transmit certificate enrollment and renewal requests to servers using
Enrollment over Secure Transport (EST), as described in RFC 7030.  It is
not normally run interactively, but it can be for troubleshooting
purposes.

When called by \fIcertmonger\fR, \fIest\-submit\fR uses the server's
\fIcacerts\fR interface to retrieve copies of the CA's certificates, and
submits signing requests using either its \fIsimpleenroll\fR or its
\fIsimplereenroll\fR interface.

A first enrollment is made with \fIsimpleenroll\fR.  If the server
requires the client to authenticate itself, HTTP Basic authentication is
used.  The password is the challenge password given to \fBgetcert
request\fR using its \fB\-L\fR or \fB\-l\fR option, or, if none was given,
the contents of the file named with the \fB\-W\fR option.  The user name is
the one given with the \fB\-U\fR option.  If no user name was given and the
password contains a colon (":"), the part before the first colon is used as
the user name, and the rest as the password.  Note that \fIcertmonger\fR
also includes the challenge password in the signing request.

Once a certificate has been issued, it is renewed using
\fIsimplereenroll\fR, and the client authenticates to the server using TLS
client authentication with the current certificate and its private key,
without a password.  The private key can be stored in a PEM file, in an NSS
database, or in a token which is accessible through an NSS database, such
as a hardware token or a TPM.  Keys in NSS databases and tokens are used
through OpenSSL's PKCS#11 provider (\fIpkcs11\-provider\fR), which
\fIest\-submit\fR loads and configures by itself, using the PKCS#11 module
which NSS uses for the key.  If the current certificate has expired, or
if the server refuses to renew it and a password is available, a new
enrollment is attempted using \fIsimpleenroll\fR.

If the server replies that the request has been accepted but is waiting
for approval, \fIest\-submit\fR tells \fIcertmonger\fR to resubmit the same
request after the delay suggested by the server's \fIRetry\-After\fR
header.

When run interactively, the signing request which is to be submitted
should be a PEM\-encoded PKCS#10 request in a file whose name is given as
an argument.  Re\-enrollment then needs the \fBCERTMONGER_CERTIFICATE\fR,
\fBCERTMONGER_KEY_STORAGE_TYPE\fR and \fBCERTMONGER_KEY_STORAGE_LOCATION\fR
variables to be set in the environment, as \fIcertmonger\fR does.

.SH MODES
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-retrieve\-ca\-certificates\fR
\fIest\-submit\fR will issue a \fIcacerts\fR request to the server and print
the CA's certificates.  This mode is used when \fIcertmonger\fR asks the
helper for the CA's root certificates.
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-enroll\fR
\fIest\-submit\fR will issue a \fIsimpleenroll\fR request to the server.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-reenroll\fR
\fIest\-submit\fR will issue a \fIsimplereenroll\fR request to the server.
.TP
If no mode is specified, \fIest\-submit\fR re\-enrolls if it has been given
a certificate which is still valid and the location of its key, and
otherwise enrolls.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-u\fR \fIURL\fR, \fB\-\-url\fR=\fIURL\fR
The location of the EST server, which must be an \fIhttps\fR URL, for
example \fIhttps://\fBSERVER\fP/\fR or
\fIhttps://\fBSERVER\fP:\fBPORT\fP/.well\-known/est\fR.  If the URL does
not include the \fI/.well\-known/est\fR path, it is appended.  This option
is always required.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR \fILABEL\fR, \fB\-\-label\fR=\fILABEL\fR
The label which the server uses to tell one of the CAs which it serves from
another.  It is added to the path, as in
\fI/.well\-known/est/\fBLABEL\fP/simpleenroll\fR.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR \fIFILE\fR, \fB\-\-cacert\fR=\fIFILE\fR
The location of the certificate(s), in PEM form, of the CA which issued
the EST server's certificate.  Only these certificates are trusted when
verifying the server's certificate.  If this option is not given, the
system's trusted CA certificates are used.
.TP
\fB\-U\fR \fINAME\fR, \fB\-\-username\fR=\fINAME\fR
The user name to use for HTTP Basic authentication when enrolling.
.TP
\fB\-W\fR \fIFILE\fR, \fB\-\-password\-file\fR=\fIFILE\fR
The location of a file containing the password to use for HTTP Basic
authentication when enrolling, if no challenge password was given for the
request.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increases the logging level.  Use twice for more logging.  This option
is mainly useful for troubleshooting.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
if the certificate was issued.  The certificate will be printed in
PEM\-encoded form.
.TP
1
if the CA is still thinking.  A cookie (state) value will be printed.
.TP
2
if the CA rejected the request, or the client's credentials.  An error
message may be printed.
.TP
3
if the CA was unreachable, or its certificate could not be verified.  An
error message may be printed.
.TP
4
if critical configuration information is missing.  An error message may be
printed.
.TP
5
if the CA is still thinking.  A suggested poll delay (specified in seconds) and
a cookie (state) value will be printed.

.SH BUGS
Please file tickets for any that you find at https://codeberg.org/freeipa/certmonger/issues

